          # 1 environment — not inside tmux, no register, NO SUCH DIRECTORY —
          # and 2 refusal — an argument error, a name a window cannot take, a
          # lane live in another window. In both, nothing was renamed, nothing
          # was written and Claude never started. Every other status came from
          # a Claude that lane-start exec'd, and is this launcher's status too;
          # so is 0, which is also how a lane-start that DECLINED the confirm
          # question reports having launched Claude bare itself. Starting a
          # second Claude behind either would be worse than the dead end this
          # catches.
          #
          # 1 and 2 are also statuses a Claude can return on its own, and the
          # window is what tells the two apart: taking a lane renames the
          # window to it (Amendment 5(f)), and a lane-start that never took one
          # left the name alone. That read is EXACT wherever the window was not
          # already the lane's — the `--lane`, record and no-window paths, which
          # is every path a restart can close a window on. Where the window was
          # ALREADY the lane (the window default, Amendment 11(2)) the rename
          # is a no-op and the read cannot separate them: there the status is
          # handed back, because the launcher is a command in an existing
          # window's shell there and a refusal returns the operator to the
          # prompt with lane-start's own words above it — while a bare Claude
          # started behind a session that may still be the lane's would mint a
          # new transcript in the lane's own window, which is the lineage fault
          # the whole protocol exists to prevent.
          #
          # AND THE WINDOW-ALREADY-THE-LANE'S ARM STOPS AT THE ONE PLACE IT
          # WOULD EXIT A PANE (`R-A11-4`: "no path exits the pane"). Where this
          # process is the only command of its tmux pane, handing a refusal back
          # is not "returning the operator to a prompt": there is no prompt
          # behind it, and tmux prints `[exited]` over a window with no Claude
          # in it, which is Evidence 2 exactly. Act 1 step 3 now names that
          # window for the lane at birth and step 2 reuses a window that already
          # carries the name, so this arm became reachable there in a way it was
          # not before, through a door act 1 itself opened. The lineage
          # objection does not reach it either: a window this launcher just
          # created, or one whose pane it just respawned, has nothing of the
          # lane's running behind it to mint a second transcript beside.
          #
          # `lane_is_pane_root` is that test, and since `R-A11-16` it is a test
          # about the PANE and not only about the environment marker act 1
          # sets: the window whose only command is `pclaude` and which this
          # launcher did not create — RV-W3's open case — is now inside the
          # fence, because the pane's own root process says so.
          if [[ "$lane_status" -eq 0 ]] \
            || { [[ "$lane_status" -ne 1 && "$lane_status" -ne 2 ]]; } \
            || { ! lane_is_pane_root && lane_window_is "$lane"; }; then
            exit "$lane_status"
          fi
          # lane-start's own refusal is already on the terminal: it is never
          # captured here, so nothing swallows it and nothing repeats it. This
          # is the launcher's one line beside it (F-W3), and it says what
          # happens next.
          lane_note_no_lane "lane-start did not take $lane (exit $lane_status); starting Claude with no lane in this window${lane_dir_note:+; $lane_dir_note}"
